WHAT THIS SOFTWARE DOES, AND WHOSE RESPONSIBILITY IT IS

This software sends deliberate bursts of HTTP requests to a web address that
its operator supplies, including repeated sign-in attempts using invalid
credentials. That is the point of it, and it is why the two paragraphs below
are not boilerplate.

Whoever runs it is solely responsible for having the right to test the address
they submit. Scanning a system you do not own or have written permission to
test may be unlawful in your jurisdiction, regardless of intent and regardless
of how gentle the requests are.

The results are not an audit, a penetration test, or a statement that any
system is secure. The software runs a small number of narrow checks and can
miss real problems, including in the areas it does check. Nothing it reports,
and nothing absent from what it reports, should be relied on as assurance.
